ems is  that we usually know which values will  be important because we know  the seasonalities of of the time series  so if i have a time series with  hourly data then i know that the the  time points around 24 hours ago  are important and maybe those uh  48 hours ago and maybe one week ago  so in this sense it's in for  seasonal time series it's we have a  notion of  which seasonalities are important and  then we  one approach is to just feed these  lacked values directly to the model  to make a prediction and this works very  well in many  in many applications so for instance  this uh  web traffic forecasting competition from  kaggle  was won by a neural network model  which uses these kind of lacked values  so in this sense you can argue  an attention mechanism um is maybe more  powerful  but if you already know the  seasonalities and if you already know  which  seasons seasonalities are important it's  maybe  the advantages maybe not that large  but uh nevertheless there have been  uh different experiments with uh  with attention-based models for  forecasting  and one such  model architecture that has been tried  for forecasting  is the so-called transformer model  so the transformer model was introduced  in this paper  also in the context of new of  natural language processing and the  transformer model is a model which only  uses attention  essentially there is no rnn  that runs along the sequence everything  is based on  layers and layers of attention and this  model  has has been very successful for  natural language processing and  it has in the meantime also been applied  to to forecasting problems  so in this paper the authors show  that you can apply the transformer  architecture to forecasting problems so  here  we see the what what i discussed  previously  there is a query vector a value vector  and a key vector and this is used to  combine  to decide which part the model  looks at in the history so for example  maybe if there's a certain pattern here  the model could  decide to look at similar patterns in  the history  to make the prediction  so one problem that forecasting  or time series problems have compared to  natural language processing is that  the sequences are often much longer so  in natural language processing typically  people will look at sentence sentences  sequences so a sequence of a single  sentence  um and then the sequence length is not  that long um so there may be i don't  know  um less than 100 words definitely  whereas in forecasting uh problems  you easily end up with thousands of time  points  and one problem that this um this  this then gives us that these attention  models become very expensive  uh because the when the sequence length  grows  so they become very compute intensive  and  um and memory intensive and  the authors here propose a uh what they  call lock spars  attention mechanism where  the attention mechanism does not look at  each individual value  um in the past but maybe it looks at  at closer distances in the near near  past  but the further um the further back the  values are  the uh the fewer  uh values it looks at and  another uh another problem that  the authors observed was that when when  they do this  the model is very prone to individual  outliers because  if it just looks at a single value um  that may be  maybe an anomaly for instance and then  it could could react too much to that  and so they also apply convolutions here  that  cause the convolutions to kind of  smoothen  this out a bit and to get some  contextual information  into this model  another approach that has recently been  proposed  in this paper is a  different approach this is the so-called  n-beats model and this model  uses a uses a basis expansion  analysis to generate  forecasts so this is an an interesting  model what  the model does is it it creates  so-called back casts and forecasts so  at each time point it it tries to  express the history of the  um of the time series in terms of some  basis functions so these could be  polynomials or  four-year features so it does basically  a decomposition of the  time series into some basis function and  then  it handles these it just expresses this  whole  history in terms of basis coefficients  and so this look back period here where  the back cast is generated  essentially we the model tries to  express  the history in terms of these basis  functions and then  predict the basis function or the  coefficients  for the future so this is how um  how how this looks like and um  so uh another interesting approach here  is that um it does  it applies kind of a stack of residual  models to do that so the first model  would  kind of predict um get the input of the  time series and  and handle that and then  whatever this back cast and forecast  look like  um the the back cast will be uh  subtracted from the actual  sequence to leave kind of an error  signal  um or in this in this look back period  and this is then the input  to the next layer and so  the next layer does the same thing it  tries to express  the look back period the time series in  this in this window  via this basis function  expansion and again the um  the residual so the the error signal is  propagated further  on the for the output  all these these forecasts  are then kind of summed up to give a  a general a combination of all these  individual forecasts  or of these residuals and  in addition the the authors also  apply an ensemble of these models so  this uses basically not just one of  these models but  but many and by doing so  um of course ensembling can also can  always  improve the accuracy  and the authors of authors observe that  in the m4 forecasting competition most  of the  the uh the methods that scored high  were ensemble methods and so their  approach to this is basically  to build an ensemble of these neural  networks  one maybe also interesting comment here  is that  the these residual blocks allow  the model to have many many layers so  this is a  common approach that has also been used  in computer vision for instance  where if you were to use a feed-forward  model where the input  to the next model is just to the next  layers just  layered on each other so basically you  just have a feed forward model  you usually cannot go very deep  with very deep architectures so  maybe five or six layers is the most you  can get  the problem is that your the gradients  from the  loss function that you compute have to  propagate through the  entire stack and this makes it very hard  to train these deep architectures  however if you use residuals what  happens is that  each of these blocks has a direct  connection to  the loss to the output as well as to the  input  so they all get a direct gradient  and that makes it much easier to train  deep architectures  so and in this n-beats model i think the  authors  have up to 150 layers here  which would be impossible to train with  simple feed-forward models  so we have a lot of neural network  models  so the question is maybe when to use  what  for which types of time series  so um this is  still open an open  research problem in general  but maybe the the current status could  be  summed up as follows so mlp  so just feed forward models are robust  baseline models  but they often need feature engineering  to to work but then if you have the  the right features engineered they can  give very good accuracies  rnns are the de facto standard models  for sequences  they however have some downsides in  particular  sometimes the training can be tricky  and they are not as efficient as other  model types  during training because they cannot make  use of gpus for parallelization as much  as  cnns can do  so some research in  neural networks basically advocates that  convolutional neural networks are as  accurate as  rnn's on many tasks but are  more efficient and the whole field  is is basically to some extent  moving from rnns to cnns  because of the efficiencies but  for uh yeah for many many  use cases rnns are still are still very  competitive some people also argue that  dilated rnns are just as efficient and  there are lots of hybrid  models between cnns and rnns  these transformer-based models that that  i showed before are also promising  models  and yeah i think there there will be  probably some more interesting research  in this  in this area  so we do not yet know which of these  architectures is the best  model but we will hopefully find out  so uh these  modern neural network models look great  and  seem to solve all our problems but  um there are still cases where  these models really are not well suited  for so in particular they struggle with  what we call the strategic strategic  forecasting problems  so for example if we want to predict  the overall amazon retail demand years  into the future  this is not a forecasting problem where  we have where we can use large amounts  of data  to generate forecasts there  there is too little training data  available  the training data is basically outdated  um  and we we need to put in a lot of um  maybe economic assumptions um uh  to to get a good good forecast  and in these sorts of applications where  you  just have one time series that you want  to predict and maybe  you care about this time series a lot um  classical forecasting models are often  much much better  just because um yeah there's so little  data  and if you care about the time series  you can invest a lot of time  into tuning your models on this time  series  where the neural network models really  shine  are kind of these operational  forecasting problems  so these are problems where we have  lots and lots of time series available  so for example  let's say we want to predict the demand  for each product  available at amazon then  each time series on its own is very  irregular  and the a local forecasting model  a classical forecasting model may have a  very hard time  looking at at this history and generate  anything meaningful  whereas neural network models or other  models that make use of  of these of these many time series  they can essentially learn that when  a time series when a product starts and  has this type of history  maybe when christmas is coming there  will be a peak and  this is typically how demand evolves so  this is where  these type of models really shine where  you have lots and lots of data of  similar things similar time series that  you want to predict  then the models can learn across all  these time series  how predictions how to predict a single  time series well well  so thank you for listening to this part  and  and i hope you enjoy the next part  thank you
 

